# WORKSPACE INSTRUCTIONS: WEAPONCORE / COREPARTS MOD DEVELOPMENT

## I. USER & COMMUNICATION CONTEXT
- The user is an experienced Space Engineers modder working with WeaponCore (CoreParts / CoreSystems).
- Target Framework: .NET Framework 4.8 via MDK2.
- Skip basic tutorials, or generic Space Engineers concepts.
- Provide direct, production-ready C# definition classes and SBC blocks.

## II. DIRECTORY ROLES & DATA TRUTH
- `./content/` contains all of the SE block definitions, audio, models and textures.
  - XML definitions (block definitions, SBCs) live in `./content/Data/`.
- `./CoreParts/` contains all of the WeaponCore C# script files that define this mod's weapon mechanics and behavior.
- `./CorePartsReferences/` is a local, read-only repository clone of the CoreParts codebase and official examples.
- CRITICAL: Treat `./CorePartsReferences/` as the absolute source of truth for:
  - Definition structures (`WeaponDef`, `AmmoDef`, `ArmorDef`, `PartAnimation`, etc.).
  - Enums, flag options, and valid property fields.
  - Never hallucinate properties or methods not found in the reference repository.
  - Note that commented notes sometimes contain errors, or misinterpretations from the WeaponCore dev community.

## III. DEFINITION INTEGRITY & VALIDATION
- Cross-Match Names: Ensure SubtypeIds in `.sbc` files match the block assignments inside `WeaponDef.Assignments.MountPoints`.
- Ammo & Particle Links: Cross-check that `AmmoDef` IDs match their respective `WeaponDef.HardPoint.Loading.Ammos` entries.
- Dummy Alignment: Ensure weapon dummy names in scripts match standard Space Engineers / CoreParts conventions (e.g., `detector_barrel_01`, `muzzle_projectile_01`).
- Isolation Boundary: Never suggest placing build tools, `.dll` references, or files from `./CorePartsReferences/` directly into the `./content/` folder.
